  "12-chemistry-02:Nernst equation": {
    "explanation": "A galvanic cell converts a spontaneous redox reaction into electrical work. Its measured potential depends not only on the identities of the electrodes but also on the activities of the dissolved species. The Nernst equation connects these chemical conditions with the cell voltage: E equals E-standard minus RT divided by nF multiplied by the natural logarithm of Q. In this equation, R is the gas constant, T is absolute temperature, n is the number of electrons transferred by the balanced cell reaction, F is Faraday's constant, and Q is the reaction quotient. At 298 kelvin, this relation is often expressed with a base-ten logarithm and a factor close to 0.0592 volts divided by n. Consider the zinc–copper cell. Zinc metal is oxidised at the anode and copper ions are reduced at the cathode. Increasing the concentration of zinc ions, which are products, raises Q and lowers the voltage; increasing copper-ion concentration, which is on the reactant side, lowers Q and raises the voltage. A salt bridge permits ionic migration that limits charge buildup, but the electrons themselves travel through the external wire. The positive cathode and negative anode convention applies to a galvanic cell, not every kind of electrochemical cell. In dilute classroom models concentrations are often substituted for activities; real concentrated solutions can depart from that approximation. A voltage reading is evidence about reaction driving force, not a measurement of how rapidly the cell will deliver current. Internal resistance and kinetics also matter.",
    "example": "Batteries gradually change voltage as their chemical composition changes during discharge. A Daniell cell made with unequal zinc-ion and copper-ion concentrations will show a different open-circuit voltage from a standard-concentration example. In the virtual lab, move the salt bridge into position first, then vary one ionic concentration while holding the other constant. Predict the direction of change before examining the meter; record the temperature and both concentrations so your result can be reproduced."
  },
  "11-biology-16:Nephron": {
    "explanation": "The nephron is the kidney's microscopic functional unit. It begins with a glomerulus, a tuft of capillaries enclosed by Bowman's capsule, followed by a sequence of specialised tubules. Pressure drives part of the blood plasma through a filtration barrier; water and many small solutes enter the capsule, while blood cells and most large proteins remain in the circulation. Filtration is only the first stage of urine formation. The proximal tubule reabsorbs much of the filtered sodium, water, glucose and amino acids. The loop of Henle establishes conditions that support the concentration of urine: its descending limb is relatively permeable to water, while its ascending regions transport salt and are much less permeable to water. The distal tubule and collecting duct adjust the final composition in response to hormonal signals. Antidiuretic hormone increases water permeability in parts of the collecting duct by promoting aquaporin insertion, allowing more water to be reclaimed when conditions permit. The kidney also secretes selected solutes into the tubular fluid. It is therefore inaccurate to describe urine as simply blood that has passed through a sieve. The renal medullary osmotic gradient, blood flow, tubular transport and hormones work together to determine the final urine volume and osmolality. High pressure does not necessarily mean healthy filtration, and a single simplified formula cannot diagnose kidney disease. In an educational model, students should compare how changing filtration and water permeability alters the balance of substances retained versus excreted, while keeping the model's assumptions visible.",
    "example": "After spending time outdoors on a hot day, a person may produce a smaller volume of more concentrated urine. Water loss and hormonal signals help explain this change. It is not because water molecules are simply 'blocked' at the kidney entrance: much filtered water is normally reabsorbed. In a model, increase ADH-dependent water permeability and compare urine volume with an otherwise identical trial. A real medical evaluation would require laboratory tests and clinical context."
  },
  "11-biology-14:Alveoli": {
    "explanation": "Alveoli are microscopic air spaces at the ends of the respiratory tree where gas exchange occurs. Their walls lie close to dense networks of pulmonary capillaries, producing a thin barrier across which gases can diffuse. Oxygen moves from alveolar air into blood when its partial pressure is higher in the alveoli than in the returning venous blood. Carbon dioxide normally travels in the opposite direction down its own partial-pressure gradient. These two fluxes are not caused by one gas pushing the other away; each gas follows its own gradient. Ventilation continually refreshes alveolar air, and perfusion carries blood through the capillaries. Efficient exchange depends on the coordination of both processes. Hemoglobin binds much of the oxygen entering the blood, whereas carbon dioxide is carried in several forms, including bicarbonate. The diaphragm contracts and moves downward during normal inspiration, increasing thoracic volume and reducing pressure relative to the atmosphere. Quiet expiration is largely passive because of elastic recoil. Alveolar surfactant reduces surface tension and helps prevent collapse, especially in small alveoli. Real lung function includes variable airway resistance, compliance, dead space, and unequal ventilation–perfusion matching; a classroom animation shows only selected principles. Distinguish airflow along bronchi from diffusion across the respiratory membrane. Also distinguish breathing from cellular respiration, which involves metabolic energy conversion inside cells. A diagram of expanding lungs describes ventilation, while a close-up of an alveolus explains gas exchange; both views are necessary to understand the process.",
    "example": "During exercise, breathing rate and depth usually increase while cardiac output also rises. These coordinated responses support delivery of oxygen and removal of carbon dioxide as working muscles increase their demand for ATP. In the virtual experiment, change thoracic volume to observe the direction of airflow, then inspect the separate oxygen and carbon dioxide gradients at the alveolar membrane. Treat the numbers as educational illustrations rather than a personal pulmonary-function result."
  },
  "12-biology-05:Transcription": {
    "explanation": "Transcription is the synthesis of an RNA strand using one DNA strand as a template. RNA polymerase binds to a promoter region with the help of appropriate factors, locally separates the DNA strands, and joins ribonucleotides in the five-prime to three-prime direction. The RNA sequence is complementary to the template DNA strand and closely resembles the coding strand, except that RNA uses uracil where DNA has thymine. In eukaryotes, many protein-coding genes initially produce a precursor messenger RNA that must be processed before translation. A five-prime cap and a three-prime poly-A tail are commonly added, and introns are removed by the spliceosome as exons are joined. Alternative splicing can generate different mature transcripts from a single gene. The ribosome later reads the mature messenger RNA in codons, three nucleotides at a time. Transcription itself does not translate a protein, and one nucleotide change does not always alter an amino acid: the outcome depends on its position, the genetic code, and subsequent processes. Some RNAs are functional products in their own right and are never translated. The word 'template' matters: reading the wrong DNA strand produces an incorrect RNA sequence. When using a sequence simulator, identify the orientation of the template, verify A–U and G–C pairing, and then separate the transcription step from RNA processing. Real transcription is dynamic and regulated; a drag-and-drop model is a conceptual sequence exercise, not a fully resolved molecular motor simulation.",
    "example": "Cells in different tissues contain essentially the same inherited genome yet express very different proteins. A pancreatic cell, for example, can express genes needed for hormone production that are not active in the same way in a skin cell. In the virtual sequence editor, mutate a coding position and inspect the resulting RNA and possible protein change. Then compare a mutation in a removed intron to one retained in a coding exon, noting that real splicing and regulation are more complex."
  },
  "12-biology-04:Independent assortment": {
    "explanation": "Mendel's law of independent assortment describes the distribution of alleles at different genes into gametes when the genes assort independently. For a diploid individual heterozygous at two such loci, AaBb, the four gamete classes AB, Ab, aB and ab have equal expected probabilities under the simplest model. Crossing two AaBb parents gives sixteen equally likely combinations in a four-by-four Punnett square. With complete dominance at both loci and no viability differences, the expected phenotypic proportions are nine showing both dominant traits, three showing the first dominant and second recessive trait, three showing the converse, and one showing both recessive traits. These are expectations over many offspring, not a guarantee that any sixteen seeds must display exactly that distribution. The law follows from the independent orientation of different homologous chromosome pairs during meiosis. Genes near one another on the same chromosome may be linked, and crossing over can produce recombinant combinations at a frequency below fifty percent. Therefore a 9:3:3:1 ratio should not be applied automatically to every two-trait problem. Dominance affects the relationship between genotype and phenotype; independent assortment concerns which allele combinations enter gametes. Those are different ideas. Before filling a Punnett square, write each parent's genotype and determine the possible gametes. Multiply gamete probabilities to obtain offspring-genotype probabilities, then convert them into phenotype probabilities using the stated dominance rules. Large virtual populations can illustrate expected ratios, while repeated random small samples demonstrate why observed counts fluctuate.",
    "example": "A breeder may cross pea plants that differ in seed colour and seed shape, then count the offspring across multiple generations. If the two genes assort independently, repeated large samples should approach the expected phenotype ratio, though no individual crop is required to match it exactly. Use the lab's Punnett square to predict the distribution first, then simulate several sets of one hundred descendants and compare the sampling variability."
  },
  "11-chemistry-05:Enthalpy": {
    "explanation": "Enthalpy, denoted H, is a thermodynamic state function defined as internal energy plus pressure multiplied by volume. For a process at constant pressure where the relevant work is pressure–volume work, the enthalpy change corresponds to the heat exchanged by the system. An exothermic reaction has a negative enthalpy change: the system releases energy to its surroundings. An endothermic reaction has a positive enthalpy change and absorbs energy. Enthalpy change depends on the initial and final states, not on the route by which the process occurs. This is the basis of Hess's law, which permits reaction enthalpies to be added algebraically when their chemical equations are combined. In calorimetry, the temperature rise of a known mass with a known specific heat capacity can be used to estimate heat transferred through q equals mc times the temperature change. The reaction and the solution do not normally have the same sign for heat: if the solution becomes warmer, it has gained heat while the reaction has released it. Real instruments also absorb energy, and an accurate calculation may require a calorimeter constant and heat-loss correction. At constant volume, such as in an idealised bomb calorimeter, the measured heat is connected more directly to changes in internal energy rather than constant-pressure enthalpy. Temperature and heat must not be confused: temperature reflects thermal state, while heat refers to energy transfer. A classroom model should distinguish measured temperatures from inferred reaction enthalpy and state what assumptions were made about insulation and heat capacity.",
    "example": "Instant cold packs often use dissolution processes that absorb heat from the surroundings, while hand warmers can release heat through chemical reactions. In a virtual neutralisation calorimeter, record the starting temperature, combine known solution quantities, and measure the modelled temperature increase. Calculate the heat gained by the liquid before inferring the reaction's heat with the opposite sign. Compare two trials with different concentrations while checking whether the vessel's heat capacity was held constant."
  },
  "11-chemistry-06:Equilibrium constant": {
    "explanation": "Chemical equilibrium is a dynamic condition in which the forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, so the macroscopic composition remains constant over time. It does not mean that reactant and product concentrations are equal, nor that molecules have stopped reacting. For a balanced reaction, the equilibrium constant is built from the activities of products and reactants, each raised to its stoichiometric coefficient. In dilute-solution school problems, concentrations are commonly substituted for activities to form an approximate Kc expression. At a fixed temperature the equilibrium constant has a particular value; changing initial amounts or pressure generally changes the equilibrium composition but not that constant. The reaction quotient Q uses the same expression with current, rather than equilibrium, composition. Comparing Q with K tells us the direction of net change: when Q is smaller than K, the forward process is favoured until equilibrium is reached; when Q is larger, the reverse process is favoured. Le Chatelier's principle offers a qualitative prediction of how an equilibrated system responds to a disturbance, but a mass-balance calculation provides a more precise result. Adding a catalyst changes how quickly equilibrium is approached without changing the equilibrium constant or equilibrium composition. Changing temperature can alter K because it changes the relative thermodynamic favourability of the reaction. Coloured iron–thiocyanate complexes are often used to show concentration shifts, but observed colour intensity is not itself a direct reading of K without an appropriate calibration. A valid simulation must preserve matter and compute an equilibrium consistent with its stated mass-action model.",
    "example": "In industrial chemical processes, operators may alter temperature and pressure to favour a useful product, while also considering how quickly the reaction proceeds. These goals can conflict. Try adding a reactant in an equilibrium simulator and observe the predicted new composition. Then compare with increasing the temperature: unlike merely changing concentrations, changing temperature may change the equilibrium constant itself. Predict the change in Q before pressing Run and explain the outcome using the balanced reaction."
  },
  "12-chemistry-03:Activation energy": {
    "explanation": "Activation energy describes the temperature sensitivity of a reaction rate in the Arrhenius equation. The rate constant k is often approximated by A multiplied by the exponential of minus Ea divided by RT, where Ea is activation energy, R is the gas constant, and T is absolute temperature. Raising T generally increases the fraction of molecular encounters that can access the relevant reaction pathway, increasing the rate constant for a positive activation energy. The pre-exponential factor A captures other influences, including collision frequency and orientation in simplified kinetic interpretations. A catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a different kinetic barrier and can increase both forward and reverse rates. It does not change the overall reaction enthalpy or the equilibrium constant at fixed temperature. Reaction rate, rate constant and reaction order are also distinct. A rate law is determined experimentally; reaction coefficients cannot usually be read directly as rate exponents unless the step is genuinely elementary. Plotting the logarithm of the rate constant against reciprocal absolute temperature can give an approximately straight line whose slope helps estimate activation energy over a suitable temperature range. Real mechanisms may involve multiple steps, intermediate species, transport limits and changes in the rate-determining step. Consequently a single Arrhenius line is not universal across all temperatures and conditions. In a simulation, distinguish an equation that predicts rate changes from a molecular animation used to illustrate the idea. Energetic dots or moving particles alone do not prove that a physically realistic collision model has been solved.",
    "example": "Food commonly spoils faster when kept warm than when refrigerated because many chemical and enzyme-mediated processes proceed more quickly at higher temperatures. Temperature dependence differs between reactions, so no single doubling rule is universal. In a virtual kinetics experiment, measure a modelled rate at several temperatures, plot the logarithm of the rate constant against reciprocal temperature, and estimate a slope. Compare that result with the imposed activation energy and state the simplified assumptions behind the comparison."
  },
